# Copy to .env; both backend services read it, and so do `yarn e2e` and
# `yarn dev`, which go through them.

# Where the account API keeps its data, in Npgsql's key=value form (a
# `postgres://` URL is not one it reads). This value matches compose.yaml, so
# it is the right one after `yarn db:start`.
DATABASE_URL=Host=localhost;Port=5432;Username=postgres;Password=postgres;Database=frontend_demo

# Where the browser reports errors and traces. Unset means Sentry stays off
# (see src/sentry.ts), which is what `yarn dev` and the browser tests want.
# Project: alexisregnaud/frontend-demo-app
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN=https://aa1392465e90500f985f79b4ac815529@o4509266977554432.ingest.de.sentry.io/4512137323085904

# One Sentry project per remote, if you want one. The app is three builds
# (docs/adr/0003), and by default all three report into the browser project
# above. Set a remote's DSN here and its build uploads its maps to, and its
# errors are routed to, a project of its own - named frontend-demo-<remote>,
# which has to exist in Sentry first (see vite.base.ts). An error thrown in
# the account's preview on the home page is then the account team's, wherever
# it was rendered: src/sentry-owner.ts reads the owner off the stack. Only the
# builds read these; the browser learns them from the bundle.
# Project: alexisregnaud/frontend-demo-account
# SENTRY_DSN_ACCOUNT=
# Project: alexisregnaud/frontend-demo-home
# SENTRY_DSN_HOME=

# Uploads the production source maps to Sentry on `yarn build`, so a stack trace
# names your code instead of the minified bundle. Unset means no map is even
# generated (see vite.config.ts). The same variable does the .NET half on
# `yarn api:build` - the PDBs, which carry their own sources, so an API frame
# shows the line too (see server/Api/Api.csproj). Mint one at
# https://alexisregnaud.sentry.io/settings/auth-tokens/, which issues an
# ORGANIZATION token (`sntrys_...`) - no scopes to pick, and it outlives whoever
# minted it. The browser's maps would take a personal token too; the .NET debug
# files would not, so one organization token is what covers both.
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Where the API reports errors and traces. A DIFFERENT Sentry project from the
# browser's, so a .NET stack and a React stack land in separate issue streams -
# the two halves of one request still read as ONE trace, because a trace is
# linked by its id and not by its project. Unset means the API stays off (see
# server/Api/Program.cs), which is what CI wants. Set here, it applies to
# `yarn e2e` too - Playwright starts the API with `yarn api:start`, and that
# reads this file.
# Project: alexisregnaud/frontend-demo-api
# SENTRY_DSN=

# Where the NOTIFICATIONS service reports - the second backend process, which
# the account API calls when an account changes (docs/adr/0004). Its own Sentry
# project, so its stacks are its own team's, the way a remote's are above; unset
# it falls back to SENTRY_DSN, so the demo is worth running with one DSN set.
# The project has to exist in Sentry first.
#
# Either way the TRACE is one. A browser pageload, the account API's PUT and
# this service's POST all carry the same trace id, because a trace is linked by
# its id and not by its project - which is the whole point of splitting the
# backend in a repository about observability. What tells the two services apart
# when they share a DSN is the `service` tag (server/Observability/SentrySetup.cs).
# Project: alexisregnaud/frontend-demo-notifications
# SENTRY_DSN_NOTIFICATIONS=

# Where the two backend services are. Defaults - port 3002 for the notifications
# service, and the URL the account API calls it at - match what `yarn start` and
# Playwright run, so neither needs setting locally. A real deployment sets
# NOTIFICATIONS_URL to wherever that service is, which is the backend's version
# of `remoteEntryUrl` in federation.config.ts: the address of a deployment you
# depend on, written down where you can see it.
# NOTIFICATIONS_PORT=3002
# NOTIFICATIONS_URL=http://localhost:3002

# WHICH BUILD every part reports as. The browser's release is set for you - the
# Vite plugin names it after the git commit and stamps it into the bundle - and
# this is how the API says the same thing rather than "1.0.0" forever (see
# server/Api/Program.cs). Unset is fine; the two just stop lining up.
# SENTRY_RELEASE=
